WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.417
Why do complex roots of real polynomials
always arrive in pairs? You know the

00:04.417 --> 00:08.833
factor and remainder theorems. In this
lesson we use the conjugate root theorem

00:08.833 --> 00:13.250
for polynomials with real coefficients. By
the end, Use the conjugate root theorem

00:13.250 --> 00:17.667
for real polynomials.

00:17.667 --> 00:21.497
If a polynomial has real coefficients and
a complex number is a root, then its

00:21.497 --> 00:25.328
conjugate must also be a root.

00:25.328 --> 00:28.241
The pair has sum four and product
thirteen. Those two numbers build a real

00:28.241 --> 00:31.155
quadratic.

00:31.155 --> 00:36.832
A quadratic with those roots is x squared
minus four x plus twenty nine equals zero.

00:36.832 --> 00:42.168
The roots two plus three i and two minus
three i are reflections of each other.

00:42.168 --> 00:46.144
Your turn. If three plus i is a root of a
real polynomial, what is the other root?

00:46.144 --> 00:50.120
Pause the video before you answer. The
conjugate, three minus i, must also be a

00:50.120 --> 00:54.096
root.

00:54.096 --> 00:59.388
A common mistake is to assume one complex
root can occur alone. Pause and decide

00:59.388 --> 01:04.680
whether that is right. Real coefficients
force the conjugate to be a root too.

01:04.680 --> 01:08.607
Your turn. If three plus i is a root, name
another root. Pause the video. Hint:

01:08.607 --> 01:12.533
Reflect the imaginary part.

01:12.533 --> 01:16.730
Three minus i is also a root. In a new
context, Roots two plus three i and two

01:16.730 --> 01:20.926
minus three i give x squared minus four x
plus thirteen. Rate your confidence from

01:20.926 --> 01:25.123
one to five.

01:25.123 --> 01:30.255
So: pair the conjugate roots, then use
their sum and product to rebuild the real

01:30.255 --> 01:35.387
quadratic. Next, we raise complex numbers
to powers with de Moivre.
